VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED

The logic is in the right unit (DTO formatting belongs in the mapper, not the entity), it covers all `OwnerDto` outputs via the single `toOwnerDto`, and nothing is duplicated. The problem is purely idiom/consistency with this codebase.

**Finding — the inline `expression = "java(...)"` string is a new, non-idiomatic pattern here.**

- `OwnerMapper.java:21-22` introduces the only `expression = "java(...)"` in the entire mapper package. A grep across all seven mappers shows the established convention for anything beyond plain field-to-field mapping is a `default` method (`OwnerMapper.toOwnerPageDto`, `PetMapper.toPetPageDto`). This change deviates from that convention.
- An escaped Java string in an annotation is untyped, unreadable (`\", \"`), not refactor-safe, and invisible to the compiler until MapStruct runs — exactly the maintainability drawbacks the codebase avoids by using real methods.

Suggested change — express the same logic the way the rest of the file already does, e.g. a named helper and `qualifiedByName`:

```java
@Mapping(target = "displayName", source = "owner", qualifiedByName = "displayName")
OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner);

@Named("displayName")
default String displayName(Owner owner) {
    return owner.getLastName() + ", " + owner.getFirstName();
}
```

This keeps the formatting in the mapper (the correct unit), makes it type-checked and readable, and matches the `default`-method style a maintainer of this file would expect. (If the format is ever wanted outside the mapper, a `getDisplayName()` on `Person`/`Owner` would be the alternative home — but the mapper is a defensible location, so the only real issue is the expression-string form.)

